::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If M is the midpoint of AB, what can you conclude? {
=A. AM &cong; MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two congruent parts.
~B. AB &cong; MB#The whole segment is not congruent to one part.
~C. A, M, and B form a right angle.#Midpoint describes segment lengths, not right angles.
~D. M is not on AB.#A midpoint lies on the segment.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If two angles form a linear pair, what is true? {
~A. They are always congruent.#Linear pair angles are not always equal.
=B. Their measures add to 180&deg;.#Correct. A linear pair forms a straight angle.
~C. Their measures add to 90&deg;.#That describes complementary angles.
~D. They are vertical angles.#Vertical angles are opposite, not adjacent.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason supports congruent opposite angles from intersecting lines? {
~A. Segment Addition Postulate#Segment addition concerns lengths.
~B. Definition of midpoint#Midpoint concerns segments.
=C. Vertical Angles Theorem#Correct. Vertical angles are congruent.
~D. Reflexive Property#Reflexive means an object is congruent to itself.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If ray BD bisects &angle;ABC, what is supported? {
~A. AB &cong; BC#Angle bisectors do not prove side congruence.
~B. &angle;ABC is 90&deg;#Bisecting does not make the whole angle right.
~C. D is the midpoint of AC#An angle bisector does not necessarily create a midpoint.
=D. &angle;ABD &cong; &angle;DBC#Correct. An angle bisector creates two congruent angles.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If B is between A and C, which equation represents segment addition? {
=A. AB + BC = AC#Correct. The parts add to the whole.
~B. AB + AC = BC#This adds a part and the whole.
~C. AC + BC = AB#This adds the whole and a part.
~D. AB = AC + BC#A part is not the whole plus another part.
}

